{"qid":"Q225","names":["Bosnia and Herzegovina"],"title":"Bosnia and Herzegovina","endonym":{"native":"Bosna i Hercegovina","lang":"bs"},"capsule":"Bosnia and Herzegovina declared independence from Yugoslavia on March 3, 1992, immediately triggering a war in which Bosniak, Serb, and Croat armed forces — and Yugoslav Army remnants — fought across the republic's territory until 1995; the conflict killed roughly 100,000 people and included the Srebrenica massacre of July 1995, Europe's worst atrocity since World War II. The Dayton Agreement, signed December 14, 1995, ended the war and established the current constitutional order: a weak federal state comprising two entities, the Federation of Bosnia and Herzegovina and Republika Srpska, each with extensive autonomous powers. A tripartite presidency rotates among Bosniak, Serb, and Croat members, and an international High Representative retains authority to dismiss officials and impose legislation. The country became an EU candidate in 2022 but remains constrained by ethnic power-sharing structures that impede central governance.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Territory is precisely defined by modern international borders recognized since 1992, with only the Brčko District retaining a special supervised status under Dayton arbitration.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Bosnia and Herzegovina","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bosnia_and_Herzegovina"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Dayton Agreement","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dayton_Agreement"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Bosnian War","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bosnian_War"}],"britannica":"place/Bosnia-and-Herzegovina","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Bosnia and Herzegovina","rulers":[{"qid":"Q16513879","name":"Jure Pelivan","start":1992,"end":1992,"image":null},{"qid":"Q57381","name":"Haris Silajdžić","start":1993,"end":1996,"image":"Haris Silajdžić 1995.jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Haris%20Silajd%C5%BEi%C4%87%201995.jpg?width=320","imageLicense":{"license":"CC BY 4.0","artist":"European Commission","attributionRequired":true}},{"qid":"Q4808284","name":"Hasan Muratović","start":1996,"end":1997,"image":"HasanMuratovic.jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/HasanMuratovic.jpg?width=320","imageLicense":{"license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","artist":"Elmirator","attributionRequired":true}},{"qid":"Q12636401","name":"Martin Raguž","start":2000,"end":2001,"image":"Martin Raguž 2025.jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Martin%20Ragu%C5%BE%202025.jpg?width=320","imageLicense":{"license":"CC BY 2.0","artist":"European People's Party","attributionRequired":true}},{"qid":"Q58124","name":"Zlatko Lagumdžija","start":2001,"end":2002,"image":"Zlatko Lagumdžija (2013-02-15) (cropped).jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Zlatko%20Lagumd%C5%BEija%20(2013-02-15)%20(cropped).jpg?width=320","imageLicense":{"license":"CC BY 4.0","artist":"European Commission","attributionRequired":true}},{"qid":"Q4953066","name":"Božidar Matić","start":2001,"end":2001,"image":null},{"qid":"Q359826","name":"Adnan Terzić","start":2002,"end":2007,"image":"Adnan Terzić (cropped).jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Adnan%20Terzi%C4%87%20(cropped).jpg?width=320","imageLicense":{"license":"CC BY 4.0","artist":"Georges Boulougouris","attributionRequired":true}},{"qid":"Q705229","name":"Dragan Mikerević","start":2002,"end":2002,"image":null},{"qid":"Q311882","name":"Nikola Špirić","start":2007,"end":2012,"image":"Visit of Nikola Špirić to the Euro